  "problem": "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $11≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,5)$\n- $27≡15\\,(\\text{mod}\\,7)$\n- $30≡-18\\,(\\text{mod}\\,4)$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we recall that \n\n$$\na≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)\\,⟺\\,n∣(a-b).\n$$\n\nSo, $a$ and $b$ are congruent modulo $n$ if and only if $(a-b)$ is divisible by $n$ (there is no remainder).\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true because $5∣(11-1).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(11-1)÷5=10÷5=2\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false because $7∤(27-15).$ Notice that $27-15=12,$ which is not divisible by $7.$\n\n- Statement III is true because $4\\,|\\,(30-(-18)).$ There is no remainder:\n\n$$\n(30-(-18))÷4=48÷4=12\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
